+//
+// Turn clock (turn-clock.patch, setting mtsTurnClock, default 0 = off and
+// everything below is skipped). With 1 the game clock only runs while it is
+// below a target the agents service writes, see docs/turn-clock.md:
+//
+//   mtsClockTarget.txt  (read)  one number, game seconds. The clock runs at
+//                               the normal 1/k rate while below it and holds
+//                               exactly at it. Re-read when its inode, size
+//                               or mtime changes, checked at most every 20 ms
+//                               (turnStep(), called once per main-loop pass).
+//                               A target below the clock never moves it back.
+//                               Missing = paused. A file left over from
+//                               before boot is ignored until it changes, so
+//                               the clock always boots paused.
+//   mtsClockNow.txt     (write) "<gameSeconds> <paused 0|1>", temp + rename,
+//                               on every pause/run change and at most every
+//                               100 ms while running.
+//   mtsClockGame.txt    (write) the game time, same moments; read at boot so
+//                               a restart resumes (paused) at the same game
+//                               time. First boot starts from the time-scale
+//                               clock above, continuous with the world.
+//
+// getCurrentTime() is cheap (no file access): min( target,
+// anchorGame + ( real - anchorReal ) / k ), with the anchor moved only by
+// turnStep(), and never goes backwards. bookkeepingTime() is the wall clock
+// in turn mode and the game clock otherwise, for server housekeeping that
+// must keep working while paused (login timeout, closing dead players'
+// sockets, the SAY flood gap).
 
